      "body": "Singapore payment-service obligations depend on the service and applicable legal scope, not on whether an agent or XRPL submits the transaction. MAS describes the Payment Services Act as regulating payment-service providers and payment systems. A token's foreign regulatory status does not establish a Singapore application's permission to provide a regulated service. [1] [2] [3]\n\nMAS's September 2026 stablecoin announcement describes consultation on legislative amendments, including the framework for qualifying MAS-regulated stablecoins. It covers Singapore-issued single-currency stablecoins pegged to SGD or a G10 currency and discusses proposals concerning foreign issuance. A consultation proposal should not be reported as a licence already granted to a particular issuer. [1] [2] [3]\n\nFor a builder, my recommendation is to map the actual activities: custody, exchange, transfer, issuance and who serves which customers. Associate each activity with the responsible entity and applicable legal review. Do not label a stablecoin MAS-regulated solely because it tracks USD, runs on XRPL or has supervision elsewhere. Keep product claims bounded by dated official evidence. [1] [2] [3]\n\nBefore deployment, prepare a service-flow description that identifies asset control and customer relationships. Check current legislation, MAS guidance and the relevant entity's permissions against that flow. These are compliance-scoping recommendations rather than a legal conclusion about this project. The article does not identify an approved bank partner, promise a regulatory exemption or claim that a successful testing transaction demonstrates production authorization. [1] [2] [3]",
